  "name": "An Investigation of the Herring Egg on Seaweed Fishery in Prince William Sound, Alaska: Estimates of Standing Crop, Growth Rates of Kelps and Patterns of Recolonization in Harvested Areas",
  "description": "This report references behavior data of fish species (Clupea pallasi); growth data of algal species (Laminaria spp.); habitat utilization, population abundance, and species composition data of algal species: Fucus spp., Laminaria spp., and Zostera marina; and production data of algal species: Clupea pallasi, Fucus spp., and Laminaria spp. from Prince William Sound, Alaska. Referenced areas of Prince William Sound include: Bidarka Point, Boulder Bay, Graveyard Point, Tatitlek Narrows, and Virgin Bay. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Rosenthal, R. J. 1978. An Investigation of the herring egg on seaweed fishery in Prince William Sound, Alaska: Estimates of standing crop, growth rates of kelps and patterns of recolonization in harvested areas. Alaska Coastal Research, Job No. OCEAN-04-78. Alaska Department of Fish and Game, Division of Commercial Fisheries. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #837 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23566150",
